  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced vocational training for increased employability of the target groups.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">While significant progress was made on national social protection programmes and safety nets, social protection is not yet comprehensive enough to provide adequate coverage for all and protect an already impoverished population from extreme poverty and recurrent shocks. To implement R208 on quality apprenticeship programme Structured apprenticeship programmes are increasingly recognized as key policy tools to facilitate the transitions of young people from school to employment. In addition, apprenticeship programmes give apprentices a chance to demonstrate their productivity potential to employers who may otherwise be reluctant to hire fresh graduates. For all these reasons, they can be a key strategy to improve young people&#x2019;s access to decent job opportunities however faces the challenges of the industries to absorb the number of young people into their workplaces due to limited resources or capacity to handle such numbers. The implementation of the Country Programme Outcome is focusing on leveraging economic and social policy measures to assist country pivot towards an inclusive and resilient economic model driven by domestic factors, unlocking higher economic impact from investment and implementing a strategic focus on reducing chronic food insecurity, promoting labour-intensity, increasing skills and access to the variables affecting standards of living (housing, energy, finance, assets, etc.) and reducing livelihood and household vulnerability. 

The beneficiaries of this intervention include, rural youths, young men and women, cooperatives, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development, Ministry in charge of Maritime and selected Technical, Entrepreneurial and Vocational Education Training (TEVET) institutions. Partners of this intervention include Government of Mozambique through the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development, Technical, Entrepreneurial and Vocational Education and Training (TEVET) Authority, Ministry of Labour and IFAD.</narrative>
  </description>
